" (https://www.preprints.org/frontend/manuscript/214afa7ea000936ce6310d234d5e77d9/download_pub)\nciteturn3673view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 15; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.preprints.org/frontend/manuscript/214afa7ea000936ce6310d234d5e77d9/download_pub\",\"lineno\":30}); Total lines: 533\nL0@P0: Article Not peer-reviewed version\nL1@P0: Spectral Analysis of the Transfer\nL2@P0: Operator in the Period-3 Logistic\nL3@P0: Sandbox: A Dynamical Heuristic for the\nL4@P0: 3x+1 Problem\nL5@P0: Liang Wang\nL6@P0: *\nL7@P0: Posted Date: 20 March 2026\nL8@P0: doi: 10.20944/preprints202603.1652.v1\nL9@P0: Keywords: Collatz conjecture; Perron-Frobenius operator; transient chaos; transient chaos;\nL10@P0: invariant measure; symbolic dynamics\nL11@P0: Preprints.org is a free multidisciplinary platform providing preprint service\nL12@P0: that is dedicated to making early versions of research outputs permanently\nL13@P0: available and citable. Preprints posted at Preprints.org appear in Web of\nL14@P0: Science, Crossref, Google Scholar, Scilit, Europe PMC.\nL15@P0: Copyright: This open access article is published under a Creative Commons CC BY 4.0\nL16@P0: license, which permit the free download, distribution, and reuse, provided that the author\nL17@P0-1: and preprint are cited in any reuse.Article\nL18@P1: Spectral Analysis of the Transfer Operator in the\nL19@P1: Period-3 Logistic Sandbox: A Dynamical Heuristic for\nL20@P1: the 3x+1 Problem\nL21@P1: Liang Wang\nL22@P1: School of Artificial Intelligence and Automation, Huazhong University of Science and Technology, 430070, P.R.\nL23@P1: China; wangliang.f@gmail.com\nL24@P1: Abstract\nL25@P1: The Collatz (3x+1) conjecture remains a formidable enigma in number theory, largely due to the\nL26@P1: unpredictable, pseudo-random fluctuations of its discrete integer orbits. To bypass the limitations of\nL27@P1: traditional analytic number theory, this paper introduces a novel interdisciplinary paradigm by\nL28@P1: translating discrete arithmetic rules into a continuous dynamical sandbox. Specifically, we establish\nL29@P1: a rigorous topological isomorphism between the 3x+1 map and the continuous Logistic map\nL30@P1: xn+1=1−μxn\nL31@P1: 2 locked at the superstable period-3 window (μ≈1.7549). By constructing a customized\nL32@P1: Markov partition anchored at the unstable fixed point, the continuous system naturally enforces a\nL33@P1: “forbidden word 11” grammar, perfectly mirroring the arithmetic constraint that an odd operation\nL34@P1: must be followed by an even one.Furthermore, by extracting the high-precision eigenspectrum of the\nL35@P1: Perron-Frobenius transfer operator, we analytically prove a 2:1 ergodic invariant measure for\nL36@P1: contraction (even) and expansion (odd) states. Crucially, by aligning the theoretical escape rate\nL37@P1: dictated by the operator’s spectral gap with the empirical stopping-time decay of 108 large integers,\nL38@P1: we demonstrate that the 3x+1 iteration and one-dimensional dissipative transient chaos belong to\nL39@P1: the exact same Universality Class. Ultimately, this study transforms an unpredictable Diophantine\nL40@P1: equation into an inevitable thermodynamic collapse, providing a groundbreaking continuous\nL41@P1: spectral analysis framework and a potent physical heuristic for the conjecture’s global convergence.\nL42@P1: Keywords: Collatz conjecture; Perron-Frobenius operator; transient chaos; transient chaos; invariant\nL43@P1: measure; symbolic dynamics\nL44@P1: 1. Introduction\nL45@P1: 1.1. Research Background and the State of the 3x+1 Problem\nL46@P1: The Collatz conjecture (also known as the 3x+1 problem) is one of the most famous unsolved\nL47@P1: problems in number theory [1]. The map is defined on the set of positive integers ℕ: if n is even,\nL48@P1: T(n)=n/2; if n is odd, T(n)=(3n+1)/2. The problem exhibits extreme unpredictability within the\nL49@P1: discrete integer space, with numerical orbits often undergoing severe pseudo-random fluctuations\nL50@P1: and long stopping times before converging to the trivial (1,2) cycle [2]. While modern high\u0002performance computational efforts have empirically verified the conjecture up to staggering bounds\nL51@P1: (exceeding 268 ) [3], numerical exhaustion cannot substitute for a rigorous mathematical proof.\nL52@P1: Despite its simple rules, the generated orbital sequences exhibit noise-like statistical characteristics\nL53@P1: [4]. This signature of “deterministic chaos” hints at profound dynamical system laws hidden beneath\nL54@P1: the arithmetic structure.\nL55@P1: Historically, research on the 3x+1 problem has diverged into three main directions:\nL56@P1: Analytic Number Theory: Scholars have extensively utilized sieve methods, the circle method,\nL57@P1: and probabilistic models [5]. A milestone in this direction is the work by Tao (2019) [6], who proved\nL58@P1: that almost all positive integers have Collatz orbits that attain almost bounded values. However,\nL59@P1: Preprints.org (www.preprints.org) | NOT PEER-REVIEWED | Posted: 20 March 2026 doi:10.20944/preprints202603.1652.v1\nL60@P1: Disclaimer/Publisher’s Note: The statements, opinions, and data contained in all publications are solely those of the individual author(s) and\nL61@P1: contributor(s) and not of MDPI and/or the editor(s). MDPI and/or the editor(s) disclaim responsibility for any injury to people or property resulting from\nL62@P1: any ideas, methods, instructions, or products referred to in the content.\nL63@P1-2: © 2026 by the author(s). Distributed under a Creative Commons CC BY license.2 of 14\nL64@P2: traditional number-theoretic tools are fundamentally bottlenecked by the “parity problem,”\nL65@P2: rendering them insufficient to provide a universal convergence proof for all orbits over an infinite\nL66@P2: span.\nL67@P2: Dynamical Analogy: Researchers have pointed out that the 3x+1 iteration possesses transient\nL68@P2: chaos characteristics, noting behavioral similarities with unimodal maps [7]. Yet, existing literature\nL69@P2: remains largely at the level of broad structural analogy. Previous studies have failed to achieve\nL70@P2: precise parameter anchoring between the discrete map and continuous unimodal maps, nor have\nL71@P2: they constructed a systematic isomorphism extending from microscopic grammar to macroscopic\nL72@P2: statistical operator spectral theory. Consequently, they fall short of providing a foundational physical\nL73@P2: explanation for the convergence of the 3x+1 map.\nL74@P2: 1.2. The Role of Symbolic Dynamics and Methodological Innovation\nL75@P2: The third major research direction—Discrete Symbolic Dynamics—serves as the critical\nL76@P2: theoretical foundation for this study. Lagarias (1985) [8] famously demonstrated that the 3x+1 map,\nL77@P2: when extended to the ring of 2-adic integers, is topologically conjugate to the shift map. Prior to this,\nL78@P2: Böhm and Sontacchi (1978) [9] established the necessary and sufficient conditions for the existence of\nL79@P2: cycles based on parity sequences. These foundational works prove that the highly non-linear\nL80@P2: arithmetic behavior of the 3x+1 problem can be rigorously encoded into symbolic sequences.\nL81@P2: However, this avenue of research has historically been confined strictly to discrete number fields,\nL82@P2: precluding the introduction of mature continuous dynamical tools such as topological entropy,\nL83@P2: invariant measures, and Perron-Frobenius transfer operators [10].\nL84@P2: To break this analytical deadlock, this study proposes a universal methodological paradigm that\nL85@P2: transforms number-theoretic mappings into continuous dynamical systems on intervals [11].\nL86@P2: Building upon the foundation of symbolic dynamics, our research represents a core extension of the\nL87@P2: “unimodal map homology” direction. Specifically, we investigate the exact symbolic correspondence\nL88@P2: between the 3x+1 map and the classic quadratic map xn+1=r−xn\nL89@P2: 2 at the period-3 attractor in the\nL90@P2: chaotic region near r≈1.75 [12,13].\nL91@P2: Refined Topological Correspondence: Rather than stopping at macroscopic behavioral\nL92@P2: analogies, this study establishes a precise parameter correspondence and demonstrates the shift\nL93@P2: equivalence of their symbolic sequences. This provides a highly effective supplement to existing\nL94@P2: dynamical analogy research.\nL95@P2: A New Heuristic Paradigm Driven by Modern Computation: The recent explosion of data\u0002driven operator approximation methods [14] and physics-informed computational frameworks [15]\nL96@P2: provides a powerful new lens for analyzing highly non-linear dynamics. By framing the behavior of\nL97@P2: the 3x+1 iteration as a “chaotic transient + period-3 attractor” [16], we offer a concrete continuous\nL98@P2: analog. This mapping from discrete grammar to continuous orbits opens the door to modern\nL99@P2: computational physics methods [17], providing a novel heuristic explanation for the conjecture’s\nL100@P2: convergence.\nL101@P2: Focused Academic Scope: This research deliberately circumvents the computationally\nL102@P2: intractable proposition of providing a pure algebraic “proof of the 3x+1 conjecture.” Instead, it\nL103@P2: focuses purely on homology analysis within the framework of symbolic dynamics. This approach\nL104@P2: aligns with the mainstream AI for Science (AI4S) and interdisciplinary research paradigms, yielding\nL105@P2: clear and rigorously verifiable academic value.\nL106@P2: 1.3. Core Hypothesis: The Logistic Map in the Period-3 Window as a Dynamical Sandbox\nL107@P2: Building upon the established framework of symbolic dynamics, the central hypothesis of this\nL108@P2: study is that an autonomous continuous dynamical system—specifically the Logistic map\nL109@P2: xn+1=1−μxn\nL110@P2: 2 locked at the superstable period-3 window (μ≈1.7549)—can serve as an experimental\nL111@P2: sandbox highly isomorphic to the statistical characteristics of the 3x+1 problem.\nL112@P2: This specific parameter is strategically selected based on three physical motivations:\nL113@P2: Preprints.org (www.preprints.org) | NOT PEER-REVIEWED | Posted: 20 March 2026 doi:10.20944/preprints202603.1652.v1\nL114@P2-3: © 2026 by the author(s). Distributed under a Creative Commons CC BY license.3 of 14\nL115@P3: Syntactic Consistency: Under this parameter, through the distribution of its invariant measure,\nL116@P3: the system naturally enforces the “forbidden word 11” grammar, mirroring the 3x+1 algebraic rule\nL117@P3: where an odd number must necessarily be followed by an even operation.\nL118@P3: Topological Rigidity: The attractor structure of the period-3 window provides a physically\nL119@P3: meaningful and rigorous topological skeleton for the odd-even cycles observed in discrete number\nL120@P3: theory.\nL121@P3: Dissipative Nature: Because the 3x+1 problem exhibits macroscopic numerical collapse, this\nL122@P3: autonomous sandbox model, through its negative Lyapunov exponent, offers an intuitive physical\nL123@P3: corollary for proving the ultimate convergence of orbits via thermodynamic formalism [18] and the\nL124@P3: statistical mechanics of chaotic systems [19].\nL125@P3: Ultimately, this paper posits that under parity-based symbolic coding (where “odd is R, even is\nL126@P3: L”), the 3x+1 map and this continuous quadratic sandbox share a fundamentally identical\nL127@P3: dynamical structure. Through the heuristic reasoning and numerical methods detailed in subsequent\nL128@P3: sections, we will verify this continuous-discrete isomorphism across three key dimensions:\nL129@P3: Iteration Mechanism Homology: Both are shift-type symbolic dynamical systems driven by a\nL130@P3: combination of a shift operator and a folding perturbation.\nL131@P3: Periodic Structure Homology: The period-3 symbolic sequences of both systems demonstrate\nL132@P3: rigorous shift equivalence.\nL133@P3: Dynamical Behavior Homology: Both exhibit the universal trajectory of “chaotic transients” that\nL134@P3: ultimately and inevitably converge to a stable periodic attractor.\nL135@P3: 2. Materials and Methods\nL136@P3: 2.1. Establishing Topological Isomorphism: The Period-3 Sandbox and the “Forbidden Word 11”\nL137@P3: The core challenge in linking discrete number theory with continuous dynamics lies in finding\nL138@P3: a topological skeleton capable of governing the macroscopic statistical properties of the 3x+1 map.\nL139@P3: We propose that the Collatz map is not a purely random process, but rather a “single-layer\nL140@P3: absolute sieve” that imposes rigid grammatical constraints on symbolic sequences. Specifically, in the\nL141@P3: parity-based 3x+1 symbolic dynamics, since for any odd number n, (3n+1) must be even, an “odd\nL142@P3: step (expansion)” must be immediately followed by an “even step (contraction)”. In the language of\nL143@P3: symbolic dynamics, if we define “1” as expansion and “0” as contraction, this arithmetic iron law\nL144@P3: translates into the dynamical “Absolute Forbidden Word 11”.\nL145@P3: To find the corresponding system in continuous space, we refer to the MSS sequence dictionary\nL146@P3: of unimodal maps (e.g., the Logistic map f(x)=1−μx2). We need to find a specific parameter µ that\nL147@P3: naturally enforces the “forbidden 11” rule while retaining sufficient Transient Chaos to simulate the\nL148@P3: pseudo-random fluctuations of Collatz orbits, with the chaotic transient inevitably converging to a\nL149@P3: unique stable period-3 attractor. The Superstable Point of the period-3 window is the unique solution\nL150@P3: satisfying this constraint. By solving the analytic equation of the period-3 orbit μ3−2μ2+μ−1=0, we\nL151@P3: can precisely lock the parameter at μ≈1.754877.\nL152@P3: To formalize this isomorphic relationship, we construct a Customized Markov Partition based\nL153@P3: on the system’s unstable fixed point x∗, Figure 1.\nL154@P3: Definition of the Fixed Point: For the map f(x)=1−μx2 , its forward fixed point x∗ satisfies\nL155@P3: f(x∗)=x∗. In the period-3 window at μ≈1.7549, this fixed point is highly unstable, representing the\nL156@P3: watershed between system expansion and contraction. Accordingly, we define the phase space states\nL157@P3: as follows:\nL158@P3: State 0 (Contraction State / Even): xn<x∗ , corresponding to the numerically dissipative n/2\nL159@P3: operation.\nL160@P3: State 1 (Expansion State / Odd): xn≥x∗ , corresponding to the numerically expansive 3n+1\nL161@P3: operation.\nL162@P3: Preprints.org (www.preprints.org) | NOT PEER-REVIEWED | Posted: 20 March 2026 doi:10.20944/preprints202603.1652.v1\nL163@P3-4: © 2026 by the author(s). Distributed under a Creative Commons CC BY license.4 of 14\nL164@P4: Figure 1. Customized Markov Partition of the 3x+1 Dynamical Sandbox. The solid line shows the Logistic map\nL165@P4: f(x)=1−μx2 locked at the period-3 window (μ≈1.7549). The red dashed line marks the boundary threshold at\nL166@P4: the unstable fixed point x∗≈0.5219 . The phase space is partitioned into State 0 (blue area, corresponding to\nL167@P4: even/contraction operations) and State 1 (red area, corresponding to odd/expansion operations). Geometrically,\nL168@P4: the image of State 1 maps entirely into State 0, enforcing the “forbidden word 11” topological rule.\nL169@P4: Topological Proof of the “Forbidden Word 11”:\nL170@P4: The validity of this sandbox model is built upon the strict proof that “transitioning from State 1\nL171@P4: to State 1 is physically impossible.” Based on the monotonically decreasing nature of the parabola\nL172@P4: f(x)=1−μx2 on the interval ሾx∗,1] (i.e., the State 1 region), the image set of any mapped point\nL173@P4: x∈ሾx∗,1] must fall within the interval ሾf(1),f(x∗)].\nL174@P4: Since f(1)=1−μ≈−0.7549 and f(x∗)=x∗≈0.5219, the entire image set of State 1 falls completely\nL175@P4: within the ሾ−0.7549,0.5219] range. Crucially, because f(1)≪x∗, the subsequent state of State 1 must\nL176@P4: inevitably be State 0. This topological constraint strictly forbids the appearance of the sequence “11”\nL177@P4: from the bottom up, thereby perfectly replicating the arithmetic skeleton of the 3x+1 map: namely,\nL178@P4: after an odd number n undergoes the (3n+1)/2 logic, the result must proceed to an even operation.\nL179@P4: By anchoring the sandbox model at the period-3 superstable point and utilizing the x∗ partition,\nL180@P4: we have created a deterministic autonomous system that is isomorphic to the Collatz conjecture in\nL181@P4: both statistics and grammatical logic. This enables us to transcend the limitations of discrete iteration\nL182@P4: and leverage the power of Transfer Operators to analyze the underlying measure and decay rate of\nL183@P4: the system.\nL184@P4: 2.2. Autonomous System Characteristics: Scale Invariance and the Static Feature of μ\nL185@P4: When constructing the 3x+1 dynamical sandbox, the system exhibits prominent Autonomous\nL186@P4: System characteristics. Unlike models requiring parameter adjustments over time or evolutionary\nL187@P4: steps, the physical laws of the 3x+1 map possess a high degree of constancy across the entire\nL188@P4: domain.\nL189@P4: Scale Invariance of Arithmetic Laws: The microscopic arithmetic laws of 3x+1 are absolutely\nL190@P4: stable. Regardless of the scale of the integer n (even as it approaches infinity), its modulo-2\nL191@P4: remainder distribution (even-odd probability) always remains 1:1 in macroscopic statistics, and the\nL192@P4: state transition probabilities after dividing by 2 or executing (3n+1)/2 do not change with the order\nL193@P4: of magnitude.\nL194@P4: Decisiveness of the Static Parameter μ: Since the “physical laws” of 3x+1 are constant across\nL195@P4: the entire domain, the dynamical sandbox isomorphic to it is an autonomous system. This means the\nL196@P4: control parameter µ does not need to evolve with iteration steps but can remain locked at the specific\nL197@P4: topological fixed point of μ≈1.7549.\nL198@P4: Preprints.org (www.preprints.org) | NOT PEER-REVIEWED | Posted: 20 March 2026 doi:10.20944/preprints202603.1652.v1\nL199@P4-5: © 2026 by the author(s). Distributed under a Creative Commons CC BY license.5 of 14\nL200@P5: Advantages of Spectral Analysis: This static feature of the autonomous system allows us to\nL201@P5: directly utilize the static Transfer Matrix (Perron-Frobenius Operator) to analyze the system’s\nL202@P5: eigenspectrum.\nL203@P5: Through this approach, researchers can focus on the invariant measure and Spectral Gap of the\nL204@P5: system, quantitatively explaining the macroscopic statistical behavior of 3x+1 orbits from a\nL205@P5: dynamical perspective by analyzing the eigenvalue distribution of static operators. This deterministic\nL206@P5: static map provides a solid physical benchmark for parsing the stability of discrete number-theoretic\nL207@P5: sequences.\nL208@P5: 3. Results\nL209@P5: 3.1. Numerical Verification of the Continuous Sandbox\nL210@P5: This section aims to demonstrate, through large-scale Monte Carlo numerical simulations, how\nL211@P5: the continuous dynamical sandbox naturally manifests the core statistical features of the 3x+1 map\nL212@P5: without relying on any number-theoretic arithmetic rules—solely through geometric folding and\nL213@P5: phase space evolution. We will establish the topological isomorphism between the continuous system\nL214@P5: and the discrete number theory problem from three dimensions: micro-grammar, transient lifetime\nL215@P5: distribution, and macroscopic steady-state measure.\nL216@P5: 3.1.1. Model Construction and Customized Markov Partition\nL217@P5: When dealing with the symbolic dynamics of unimodal maps, traditional methods usually use\nL218@P5: the critical point (extremum xc=0) as the boundary for the generating partition. However, to replicate\nL219@P5: the microscopic arithmetic skeleton of the 3x+1 rules, we abandon this conventional approach.\nL220@P5: Instead, targeting the Logistic map f(x)=1−μx2 in the period-3 window (μ≈1.7549), we propose a\nL221@P5: customized non-generating partition based on the forward unstable fixed point x∗.\nL222@P5: By solving the equation 1−μ(x∗)2=x∗, we determined the position of the unstable repellor in the\nL223@P5: first quadrant to be x∗≈0.5219. Using this topological singularity as a threshold, we strictly partition\nL224@P5: the phase space [−1,1] into two state intervals (Figure 2):\nL225@P5: State 0 (Contraction State / Corresponding to Even): x∈[−1,x∗). Most of this interval is near the\nL226@P5: origin, corresponding to dynamical dissipation and contraction (smaller absolute derivative).\nL227@P5: State 1 (Expansion State / Corresponding to Odd): x∈[x∗,1]. This interval is near the edge of the\nL228@P5: phase space, corresponding to momentary dynamical expansion (larger absolute derivative).\nL229@P5: The physical significance of this customized partition is that it is not intended to maximize the\nL230@P5: differentiation of initial orbital conditions, but rather to geometrically align perfectly with the odd\u0002even operation mechanism in the 3x+1 problem.\nL231@P5: Preprints.org (www.preprints.org) | NOT PEER-REVIEWED | Posted: 20 March 2026 doi:10.20944/preprints202603.1652.v1\nL232@P5-6: © 2026 by the author(s). Distributed under a Creative Commons CC BY license.6 of 14\nL233@P6: Figure 2. Establishment of the Topological Skeleton of the Dynamical Sandbox. The intersection of the Logistic\nL234@P6: map f(x)=1−1.7549x2 and the diagonal y=x determines the forward unstable fixed point x∗≈0.5219 . This\nL235@P6: singularity, marked by the red dashed line, serves as the partition threshold, dividing the phase space into State\nL236@P6: 0 (blue area) and State 1 (red area).\nL237@P6: 3.1.2. Numerical Experiment 1: Micro-Grammar and Entropy Collapse\nL238@P6: To verify whether the sandbox model possesses the arithmetic grammar of 3x+1, we iterated\nL239@P6: the system with random initial values and calculated the transition probabilities between State 0 and\nL240@P6: State 1. The statistical emergence of this grammar and its thermodynamic consequences are\nL241@P6: illustrated in Figure 3.\nL242@P6: Natural Emergence of the “Forbidden Word 11”: The 2ൈ2 empirical Markov transition matrix\nL243@P6: obtained experimentally shows that the transition probability P(1→1)≡0 (Figure 3, left). This is\nL244@P6: easily proven via geometric analysis: due to the monotonically decreasing nature of the parabola in\nL245@P6: the [x∗,1] interval, the maximum mapped value for all points in State 1 is f(x∗)=x∗ , and the\nL246@P6: minimum is f(1)=1−μ≈−0.7549. Therefore, the image set of State 1 falls entirely within the interval\nL247@P6: of State 0. This topological constraint strictly forbids consecutive State 1s, perfectly mirroring the\nL248@P6: algebraic iron law of the 3x+1 map: an odd number operation must inevitably be followed by an\nL249@P6: even operation.\nL250@P6: Collapse of Information Entropy: Due to the constraint of the absolute forbidden word 11, the\nL251@P6: binary symbolic sequence generated by the system is no longer a uniform random distribution.\nL252@P6: Calculations show that if the states are treated as a random coin-flipping process, the initial\nL253@P6: unrestricted Shannon entropy would be 1.0. However, constrained by the topological grammar of\nL254@P6: the mapping, the system’s symbolic sequence Shannon entropy irreversibly collapses from 1.0 to\nL255@P6: approximately 0.667 (Figure 3, right). This entropy reduction process quantitatively delineates the\nL256@P6: system’s convergence from a completely random state to a highly ordered, parity-constrained state.\nL257@P6: Preprints.org (www.preprints.org) | NOT PEER-REVIEWED | Posted: 20 March 2026 doi:10.20944/preprints202603.1652.v1\nL258@P6-7: © 2026 by the author(s). Distributed under a Creative Commons CC BY license.7 of 14\nL259@P7: Figure 3. Microscopic Grammar Emergence and Information Entropy Collapse. Left: Heatmap of the empirical\nL260@P7: transition probability matrix, demonstrating that P(1→1)=0. Right: Shannon entropy as a function of iteration\nL261@P7: steps, illustrating a rapid collapse from an initial value of 1.0 to a stable asymptote near 0.68.\nL262@P7: 3.1.3. Numerical Experiment 2: Macro-Dynamics and Long-Tail Stopping Time\nL263@P7: The most famous macroscopic feature of the 3x+1 problem is its highly unpredictable Stopping\nL264@P7: Time and overall exponential decay pattern. In the sandbox model, we compared the transient\nL265@P7: behavior of continuous trajectories with real large integers.\nL266@P7: We conducted a side-by-side comparison between the stopping time distribution of real large\nL267@P7: integers (randomly drawn from a positive integer sequence on the order of 107) under the 3x+1 rule\nL268@P7: and the number of iteration steps required for a massive amount of random initial points x0∈[−1,1]\nL269@P7: to fall into the attractor in the sandbox model. Both systems exhibited highly typical Right-skewed\nL270@P7: Long-tail distributions, shown in Figure 4.\nL271@P7: The isomorphism of this feature holds profound dynamical implications: it signifies that the\nL272@P7: discrete jumps of real large integers and the continuous folding of real numbers in the sandbox both\nL273@P7: macroscopically undergo a highly similar roaming period of “Transient Chaos”. Before being\nL274@P7: captured by the final absorbing state, particles perform a long, random-walk-like roaming near the\nL275@P7: Chaotic Repellor inside the phase space, thereby forming statistically highly consistent long-tail\nL276@P7: lifetime distributions.\nL277@P7: Figure 4. Decisive Experimental Evidence of Universality Class Consistency. Illustration suggestions: Side-by\u0002side subplot comparison. Left panel: Stopping time distribution histogram for real large integers in the 3x+1\nL278@P7: Preprints.org (www.preprints.org) | NOT PEER-REVIEWED | Posted: 20 March 2026 doi:10.20944/preprints202603.1652.v1\nL279@P7-8: © 2026 by the author(s). Distributed under a Creative Commons CC BY license.8 of 14\nL280@P8: problem (logarithmic y-axis preferred to show the exponential tail). Right panel: Lifetime distribution histogram\nL281@P8: of random initial values in the Logistic sandbox model (μ=1.7549). Both should display a highly similar right\u0002skewed long-tail contour.\nL282@P8: 3.1.4. Numerical Experiment 3: Ergodicity and the 2:1 Invariant Measure\nL283@P8: To observe the ultimate fate of the system, we removed the transient phase data and recorded\nL284@P8: the invariant probability density ρ(x) formed in the phase space after millions of iterations.\nL285@P8: After prolonged evolution, the smooth initial probability density completely collapsed, and a\nL286@P8: stunning geometric landscape emerged in the phase space (Figure 5): three sharp Delta peaks (Dirac\nL287@P8: delta spikes). The coordinates of these three peaks precisely correspond to the three poles of the\nL288@P8: period-3 attractor: x=0, x=1, and x≈−0.7549. Its cycle path is 0→1→−0.7549→0.\nL289@P8: At this point, we integrated over the phase space based on the threshold x∗ defined in Section\nL290@P8: 3.1:\nL291@P8: The peaks at x=0 and x≈−0.7549 are both located to the left of the threshold red line (State 0).\nL292@P8: The peak at x=1 is uniquely located to the right of the threshold red line (State 1).\nL293@P8: Since the dynamical orbit resides on these three points with equal probability, simple geometric\nL294@P8: integration directly proves that the resident measure of State 0 (contraction/even) accurately occupies\nL295@P8: 66.67% (i.e., 2/3) of the whole, while the resident measure of State 1 (expansion/odd) accurately\nL296@P8: occupies 33.33% (i.e., 1/3).\nL297@P8: Figure 5. Invariant Measure and the 2:1 Ergodic Distribution. The probability density histogram ρ(x) on the\nL298@P8: phase space x∈[−1,1] displays three sharp Dirac delta spikes corresponding to the poles of the period-3\nL299@P8: attractor. The vertical red dashed line marks the partition threshold x∗≈0.5219. Integration over these regions\nL300@P8: yields a strict 2:1 measure ratio (2/3 for State 0 on the left, 1/3 for State 1 on the right), visually confirming the\nL301@P8: statistical parity distribution of the 3x+1 map.\nL302@P8: Under the condition of assuming no number-theoretic prerequisites, using pure geometric\nL303@P8: folding and the invariant measure of continuous systems, this result perfectly validates the long\u0002standing conclusion in discrete number theory—that in the random walk of 3x+1, the frequency of\nL304@P8: occurrence of even operations is bound to be twice that of odd operations. This not only proves their\nL305@P8: Universality Class connection in statistical physics but also lays a solid phenomenological foundation\nL306@P8: for subsequently using transfer operators to construct a rigorous analytical proof for the system.\nL307@P8: Preprints.org (www.preprints.org) | NOT PEER-REVIEWED | Posted: 20 March 2026 doi:10.20944/preprints202603.1652.v1\nL308@P8-9: © 2026 by the author(s). Distributed under a Creative Commons CC BY license.9 of 14\nL309@P9: 3.2. Spectral Analysis and Universality Verification\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn3673view1 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://assets-eu.researchsquare.com/files/rs-9024307/v1_covered_a1136c72-f4ab-4752-a0b8-7690b3aea3bc.pdf\",\"lineno\":0}); Total lines: 1\nL0: Failed to fetch https://assets-eu.researchsquare.com/files/rs-9024307/v1_covered_a1136c72-f4ab-4752-a0b8-7690b3aea3bc.pdf: (403) Forbidden\n"
